    "knowledge_point_id": "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a",
    "knowledge_point": "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial",
    "problem": "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{6}-7{x}^{2}+x-6,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?",
    "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)",
